    "body": "When testing a hardware wallet integration, I found that when attempting to create a wallet with the wrong app open on the hardware wallet, the GUI crashes instead of showing an error message. After restarting, when attempting to create a wallet with the same name again, it fails with \"Database already exists\". Over RPC, there's no crash, but `createwallet` returns error code -1 and leaves the same files and directories behind.\n\nThis happens when setting up an external-signer wallet. When the signer fails, setting up the wallet throws `std::runtime_error`, and `CreateWallet()` doesn't catch it. The GUI's worker thread has no handler, so the uncaught exception ends in `std::terminate`, and RPC's catch-all turns it into error code -1. The directory and `wallet.dat` are created before setup and never removed when setup fails.\n\nThis uncaught exception has been encountered previously in bitcoin-core/gui#395, and bitcoin-core/gui#396 stopped the dialog from enabling the \"External signer\" option, but didn't handle the exception.\n\n\nCommit 1 catches the exception as `LoadWalletInternal()` does. The GUI shows an error dialog, and RPC returns error code -4, with the message starting with \"Wallet creation failed.\"\nCommit 2 ensures that on failure, it removes only what was created. The cleanup also runs for failures that don't throw, e.g. an invalid `-addresstype`.\n\nHow to reproduce:\n```\ncmake -B build -DBUILD_GUI=ON && cmake --build build\nbuild/bin/bitcoin-qt -regtest -datadir=$(mktemp -d) -signer=\"python3 test/functional/mocks/invalid_signer.py\"\n```\n\n\nThen click File, Create Wallet, Create. \"External signer\" is already ticked and the name is pre-filled as `trezor_t`.\n\nOn master: `terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::runtime_error'`, the process aborts, and `trezor_t/wallet.dat` is left behind.\nWith the PR: a \"Create wallet failed\" dialog appears, a retry shows the same dialog, and nothing is left on disk.\n\nThe tests `CreateWalletSignerFailure` and `CreateWalletInvalidArgs` in `wallet_tests` create wallets through the wallet loader, which is the same call the GUI makes. `wallet_signer.py` now expects -4, and checks that a failed creation leaves nothing behind, that a retry fails the same way, and that a directory or files that already existed are kept.\n\nYou can see the tests fail without the fix:\n\n```\ngit checkout HEAD~2 -- src/wallet/wallet.cpp\ncmake --build build\nbuild/bin/test_bitcoin --run_test=wallet_tests/CreateWalletSignerFailure,CreateWalletInvalidArgs\nbuild/test/functional/wallet_signer.py\n```",
